Attention Is All You Need

Attention Is All You Need is a research paper published in 2017 by Vaswani and his team at Google.
The paper introduces a new model called the Transformer.
The Transformer is mainly used for language translation and other sequence tasks.
Earlier models used RNNs to handle sequences, but the Transformer does not use RNNs.
It also does not use CNNs for sequence processing.
The main idea of the paper is that attention alone is enough to understand sequences.
The Transformer uses a technique called self-attention.
Self-attention helps the model understand how words in a sentence are related to each other.
It can easily learn long-distance relationships between words.
The Transformer has two main parts: an encoder and a decoder.
The encoder reads the input sentence and understands its meaning.
The decoder produces the output sentence step by step.
Each layer of the model contains attention layers and feed-forward neural networks.
The model uses multi-head attention to focus on different parts of the sentence at the same time.
To make attention calculations stable, scaled dot-product attention is used.
Since the model has no order by default, positional encoding is added to show word order.
Positional encoding helps the model know which word comes first or later.
The Transformer can process many words at the same time, unlike RNNs.
This makes training faster and more efficient.
The Transformer gave better results in machine translation tasks.
Many modern models like BERT, GPT, and T5 are based on this architecture.
Today, Transformers are widely used in chatbots, translation, and text generation.
The paper changed how Natural Language Processing (NLP) models are built.
